00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
french police escorted salah abdeslam to  paris's high court for the start of the  trial into the 2015 paris attacks  wearing a black t-shirt his hair long  abduslam sat with 13 of his co-accused  in a specially built courtroom abduslam  is believed to be the only surviving  attacker he fled the city after the  killings four months later he was  arrested in brussels  when a judge asked him to confirm his  identity abdeslam said  first i want to testify that there is no  other god than allah and muhammad is his  messenger the judge replied we'll talk  about that later abduslam went on to say  that he had given up work to fight for  isil  it was nearly six years ago that 10  attackers rampaged across paris killing  people in cafes the bataclan concert  hall and outside france's national  sports stadium by the end of the night  130 people were dead isil claimed  responsibility the french president  declared a state of emergency across  france there was shock and grief  hundreds of people are expected to  testify at the trial that's scheduled to  last nine months for the relatives of  the victims and survivors it is likely  to be an emotional and harrowing ordeal  we first met muhammad amgar a few months  after the attacks he was working outside  the stadium when an attacker blew  himself up muhammad was badly injured  now he says he wanted to be in the court  to see the accused they look like  they're afraid you know they're afraid  to be healed they look like they lost  are you scared to come here a scale of  what  i don't think i can be scared because  for me like i said long time ago each  day is extra day for me  other victims though decided to stay  away  index  the suffering is unspeakable it is  inexpressible what they expect is for  justice to be particularly firm  the acts of which the defendants accuse  the particularly monstrous acts  abduslam has so far refused to cooperate  with french police he may choose not to  testify during the trial  some hope that he will break his silence  and shed more light on what happened and  why either way after so many years most  victims hope that justice will finally  be served natasha butler al jazeera  paris
 

